+\paragraph{How strong is the explicit chart?}
+Definition~\ref{def:input-contract} asks for the structure constants of the Lazard Lie algebra. Three common presentations reduce to it.
+\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*),leftmargin=2em]
+  \item \emph{Unitriangular matrix groups.} Let $G \le \UT_n(\Fp)$ be given by generator matrices $g_1,\dots,g_s$ and an oracle $f$ on matrices, and suppose that the non-unital associative algebra $\mathcal{N}$ generated by the $g_i - I$ satisfies $\mathcal{N}^p = 0$. This holds whenever $n \le p$, since products of $n$ strictly upper triangular matrices vanish, and in general it can be checked by linear algebra in $\poly(n,s,\log p)$. Then $G \subseteq I + \mathcal{N}$, $G$ has class below $p$, and $G$ has exponent dividing $p$, since $(I+N)^p = I + N^p = I$. The exponential and the logarithm $\log(I+N) = \sum_{k=1}^{p-1}(-1)^{k+1}N^k/k$, truncated below degree $p$, are inverse bijections between $I+\mathcal{N}$ and $\mathcal{N}$, and their denominators are prime to $p$. In the free associative algebra over $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$ modulo degree $\ge p$, the truncated Baker--Campbell--Hausdorff series also has denominators prime to $p$, so $\exp X \exp Y = \exp \mathrm{BCH}(X,Y)$ on $\mathcal{N}$; by uniqueness of the Lazard ring structure~\cite[\S10.2 and Example~10.24, p.~124]{Khukhro1998}, $\log$ restricted to $G$ is the Lazard logarithm. The Lie subalgebra $\mathcal{L}'$ generated by the $\log g_i$ equals $\log G$: it lies in $\log G$, which is a Lie subalgebra, and $\exp\mathcal{L}'$ is a subgroup containing every $g_i$. Bracket closure with row reduction gives a basis of $\mathcal{L}'$ and its structure constants in time $\poly(n,s,\log p)$, and $y \mapsto f(\exp y)$ is the oracle of Definition~\ref{def:input-contract}, at one query of $f$ per query. Theorem~\ref{thm:main} then applies when the class is fixed. This covers the groups $\Fp \ltimes \Fp^m$ of~\cite{BaconChildsVanDam2005,IvanyosSantha2015} whenever $p$ exceeds the class $c$: the generator of $\Fp$ acts by a unipotent matrix, which a flag of kernels of its powers minus $I$ conjugates to upper unitriangular form in polynomial time, and in the resulting embedding into $\UT_{m+1}(\Fp)$ products of $c+1$ of the matrices $g_i - I$ vanish, so $\mathcal{N}^{c+1} = 0$.
+  % AI-checked exactly for p = 3, m = 4, class 2 (order 243): research/audits/reviews/2026-11-08-rees-blackbox-e2e/semidirect_check.py.
