+\begin{remark}[Scope of Corollary~\ref{cor:black-box}]
+\label{rem:black-box-scope}
+The corollary is a field form of the $e = 1$ chart route in the proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:black-box}(ii) (Appendix~\ref{app:torsion-blackbox}), with a row-reduced complement in place of Lemma~\ref{lem:tb-chart}, stated with Schedule~1 alone. Like Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, it is conditional only on Theorem~\ref{thm:is-import}; its proof uses Lemmas~\ref{lem:tb-words}, \ref{lem:tb-evaluation} and~\ref{lem:tb-kernel}. Given class below $p$, the exponent-$p$ promise can be tested, since $G$ has exponent $p$ exactly when every $g_i^p = 1$. No encoding of $1$ is given, so the test checks that the elements $e_i = g_i^p$ satisfy $e_ie_i = e_i$ and $e_i = e_1$, which under unique encoding holds exactly when every $e_i$ is $1$ ($O(s\log p)$ multiplications). It makes four restrictions; Corollary~\ref{cor:black-box-pe} below removes the first, and the other three apply to it as well. (i) Exponent $p$: for exponent $p^e > p$, see Corollary~\ref{cor:black-box-pe}, which takes route~(R2) of Proposition~\ref{prop:black-box}(ii). (ii) Unique encoding: the corollary, like~\cite{ImranIvanyos2023}, assumes unique encodings. (iii) $p$-groups: for black-box nilpotent groups of class at most $c$, the composition of Theorem~\ref{thm:bb-envelope} needs, with Schedule~1 alone, beyond Corollary~\ref{cor:black-box-pe} only a Sylow split by order finding and factoring and the exact algorithm of~\cite[Theorem~1]{ImranIvanyos2023} for the primes $p \le c$; Theorem~\ref{thm:bb-envelope} is stated with the combined rule of Remark~\ref{rem:combined-schedule} and is conditional as stated there. (iv) Oracle input only: the coset-copy input of Theorem~\ref{thm:main} is not covered, since $E^{-1}$ is never computed.
+
+A finite simulation (\file{theorems/rees_blackbox_end_to_end.py}; Appendix~\ref{app:provenance}, item~\ref{prov:bb-corollary}; finite computation, AI-checked, pending human review) runs the chart step of the corollary from opaque handles on three small unitriangular matrix groups and on the free two-generator class-three group of exponent $5$ given by hidden coordinates, and then the scaled exact simulator of the algorithm of Theorem~\ref{thm:main} on the acquired chart (with the per-grade arities of Remark~\ref{rem:combined-schedule}, not Schedule~1 alone). The Fourier sampling law is computed from the fibres of $\Phi$ in floating point, and the homomorphism check on the chart is exhaustive or sampled as recorded. In every run the subgroup generated by the returned handles equals $H$. It is a finite check and proves nothing for other groups.
+\end{remark}
