+\begin{remark}[Source of the route]
+\label{rem:black-box-pe-source}
+The reduction from exponent $p^e$ to exponent $p$ is not new. Ivanyos, Sanselme and Santha reduce the HSP in any class of groups of constant nilpotency class that is closed under subgroups and quotients to $p$-groups of exponent $p$ with hidden subgroup trivial or of order $p$~\cite[Theorem~2]{IvanyosSanselmeSantha2012}: their Lemma~2 is a normalizer recursion, and their Lemma~3 passes, when $p$ exceeds the class, to the subgroup of elements of order dividing $p$ (numbering of Section~3 of the arXiv version). Their reduction is stated for groups given by refined polycyclic presentations with polynomial-time collection, which, as they note, a quantum algorithm computes for solvable black-box groups. The proof of Corollary~\ref{cor:black-box-pe} follows that route on the acquired Lazard chart. Each call recovers the whole intersection of the hidden subring with the socle of the current section, no polycyclic presentation or collection is needed, and the counts are explicit. We do not claim the route as new. Remark~\ref{rem:tb-ring-sources} relates the recursion to other outer loops. A finite computation (Appendix~\ref{app:provenance}, item~\ref{prov:bb-corollary-pe}; finite computation, AI-checked, pending human review) runs the exponent, pullback, kernel, chart and recursion steps of Corollary~\ref{cor:black-box-pe} from $40$-bit opaque handles on $48$ groups of orders $3^4$ to $3^6$, $5^4$ and $5^5$, of class $2$ to $4$ and exponent $9$ to $125$. With a brute-force oracle-only field solver in place of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, it recovers all $5359$ hidden subgroups tested within the call cap and validates every field call as an instance of Definition~\ref{def:input-contract}; the kernel readout from the exact sampling law equals $\ker\phi$ in $958$ of $960$ trials, and the public check rejects the other two. It does not exercise Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, the Fourier approximation or any cost, and proves nothing for other groups.
+\end{remark}
